tections can be jointly applied where data processing intersects with consumer interests [Data: Relationships (505); Entities (575)]. The complementarity between LGPD and CDC rights is further clarified in Artigo 64, which states that LGPD does not foreclose CDC rights, reinforcing interoperability across regimes [Data: Entities (575); Relationships (505)]. This regulatory interplay informs how penalties, transparency duties, and consumer remedies are implemented in practice. [Data: Entities (575); Relationships (505)]\\n\\nSanctions under LGPD are codified and benchmarked by authoritative sources, including benchmark references used in enforcement contexts that outline penalties such as fines up to 50 million BRL per infraction and related enforcement mechanics, with explicit guidance published by authorities [Data: Entities (47); Entities (52)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Data access routes under LGPD: ROTA 1 and ROTA 2\",\n      \"explanation\": \"LGPD defines two data-access routes to balance immediacy and transparency: Route 1 (ROTA 1) provides immediate access to basic data, while Route 2 (ROTA 2) offers a full declaration detailing data origin, processing criteria, and purposes. These routes are explicitly described in the LGPD framework and are linked to practical decision diagrams that govern access [Data: Entities (72, 73); Relationships (70)]. The existence of both routes supports flexible compliance and enhances data subjects’ ability to exercise rights while allowing controllers to balance rapid access with provenance and accountability. [Data: Relationships (70); Entities (72, 73)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Cross-regulatory interplay: LGPD-CDC complementarity\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The intersection between LGPD and CDC represents a formal cross-regulatory area where data protection and consumer rights protections overlap, creating shared accountability across regimes [Data: Entities (515); Relationships (505)]. This cross